# ComposeUX Reactive Window [SPEC:CXC-RWIN]

> A reactive window is a dormant UI template that materializes when a bus
> condition fires. One window at a time. Higher priority replaces lower.
> The window is a live bus participant — its widgets show real-time state,
> not a frozen snapshot. When the condition clears or the operator
> acknowledges, the window is destroyed and its widgets unsubscribed.

---

## 1. Concept [CXC-RWIN-CONCEPT]

The reactive window extends the bus condition evaluation model from the
tab controller (spec 08) to window lifecycle:

| Component | What It Does | Lifecycle |
|-----------|-------------|-----------|
| Tab controller | Switches between existing views on condition | Persistent — views always exist |
| **Reactive window** | **Creates a view on condition, destroys on dismiss** | **Ephemeral — view exists only while condition holds** |

The window is defined as a **template** in `.compose.yaml`. It has no visual
presence until triggered. When triggered, the template is instantiated: a
top-level window is created, child widgets are constructed and subscribed to
the bus, and the operator sees a contextual UI for the triggering condition.

**One window at a time.** If a new trigger fires while a window is showing,
priority determines the outcome: higher replaces, lower queues or drops.
This is not a window manager — it's a single reactive surface.

---

## 2. Template Definition [CXC-RWIN-TEMPLATE]

### 2.1 Config Schema [CXC-RWIN-SCHEMA]

```yaml
reactive_windows:
  - id: ammo_alert
    title: "Ammunition Status"
    trigger:
      - namespace: ammo_inventory
        condition: "< 20"
    dismiss:
      auto:
        - namespace: ammo_inventory
          condition: ">= 50"
      timeout: 300                    # auto-dismiss after 5 minutes if no clear
      acknowledge: true               # show acknowledge button (default: true)
    priority: high
    size: [400, 300]                  # width, height in pixels
    widgets:
      - id: ammo_gauge
        type: GaugeWidget
        namespace: ammo_inventory
        label: "Current Inventory"
        layout: {row: 0, column: 0, columnspan: 2}
      - id: resupply_btn
        type: ButtonWidget
        namespace: ammo_reload_request
        label: "Request Resupply"
        layout: {row: 1, column: 0}
      - id: last_engagement
        type: LabelWidget
        namespace: ammo_last_engagement
        label: "Last Engagement"
        layout: {row: 1, column: 1}

  - id: ftl_fault
    title: "FTL Drive Fault"
    trigger:
      - namespace: ftl_status
        condition: "== offline"
      - namespace: warp_core_temp
        condition: "> 200"
    dismiss:
      auto:
        - namespace: ftl_status
          condition: "== online"
        - namespace: warp_core_temp
          condition: "<= 150"
      timeout: 0                      # no auto-timeout — stays until condition clears
      acknowledge: true
    priority: critical
    size: [500, 400]
    widgets:
      - id: ftl_status_led
        type: RelayWidget
        namespace: ftl_status
        label: "FTL Status"
        layout: {row: 0, column: 0}
      - id: temp_gauge
        type: GaugeWidget
        namespace: warp_core_temp
        label: "Core Temperature"
        layout: {row: 0, column: 1}
      - id: diag_log
        type: BusMonitorWidget
        namespace: _bus/fault
        label: "Fault Log"
        layout: {row: 1, column: 0, columnspan: 2}
```

### 2.2 Field Definitions [CXC-RWIN-FIELDS]

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|-------|------|----------|-------------|
| `id` | string | Yes | Unique template identifier |
| `title` | string | Yes | Window title bar text |
| `trigger` | list | Yes | Conditions that open the window (OR — any fires) |
| `trigger[].namespace` | string | Yes | Namespace to monitor |
| `trigger[].condition` | string | Yes | Condition expression (same syntax as bindings) |
| `dismiss.auto` | list | No | Conditions that auto-close (AND — all must be true) |
| `dismiss.timeout` | int | No | Auto-dismiss after N seconds. 0 = no timeout. Default: 300. |
| `dismiss.acknowledge` | bool | No | Show acknowledge/dismiss button. Default: true. |
| `priority` | string | No | `critical`, `high`, `normal`, `low`. Default: `normal`. |
| `size` | [int, int] | No | [width, height] in pixels. Default: [400, 300]. |
| `widgets` | list | Yes | Widget definitions (same schema as module widgets) |

---

## 3. Lifecycle [CXC-RWIN-LIFECYCLE]

### 3.1 State Machine [CXC-RWIN-STATES]

```
                  trigger fires
  DORMANT ──────────────────────────► ACTIVE
     ▲                                   │
     │                                   │ dismiss (auto/timeout/ack)
     │                                   ▼
     └───────────────────────────── DISMISSED
                                   (destroy window,
                                    return to dormant)
```

All reactive window templates start in DORMANT. Only one can be ACTIVE at a time.

### 3.2 Trigger Evaluation [CXC-RWIN-TRIGGER]

The reactive window module subscribes to all trigger namespaces across all
templates. On each bus message:

1. Evaluate trigger conditions for all DORMANT templates
2. If one or more fire: select the highest-priority template
3. If no window is active: instantiate the winner
4. If a window is active: compare priorities
   - New priority > current: dismiss current, instantiate new
   - New priority <= current: drop (the current window is more important)

**Trigger conditions are OR'd**: any single condition in the template's
`trigger` list fires the window. Multiple conditions provide multiple
paths to the same alert.

### 3.3 Window Instantiation [CXC-RWIN-INSTANTIATE]

When a template activates:

1. Create a `CTkToplevel` window with the template's title and size
2. Build the widget grid inside the window from the template's widget definitions
3. Subscribe each widget to its declared namespace on the internal bus
4. If `dismiss.acknowledge` is true: add a dismiss button at the bottom
5. Publish activation event to the bus (see section 5)
6. Start the dismiss timeout timer (if configured)

The window's widgets are live — they receive bus traffic and render in real time.
The ammo gauge shows the current value, updating as it changes. This is not a
static alert — it's a contextual dashboard.

### 3.4 Dismiss Conditions [CXC-RWIN-DISMISS]

Three paths to dismissal:

**Auto-dismiss (condition clears):**
- Conditions in `dismiss.auto` are AND'd — ALL must be true to auto-dismiss
- Evaluated on each bus message while the window is active
- Example: ammo alert auto-dismisses when `ammo_inventory >= 50`

**Timeout:**
- If `dismiss.timeout > 0`, the window auto-dismisses after N seconds
- The timeout resets if the trigger condition fires AGAIN while the window is active (the condition is ongoing, not stale)
- Timeout = 0 means the window stays until explicitly dismissed or condition clears

**Operator acknowledge:**
- If `dismiss.acknowledge` is true, a dismiss button is shown
- Clicking it dismisses regardless of auto conditions
- Publishes an acknowledge event to the bus (so other systems know the operator saw it)

### 3.5 Window Destruction [CXC-RWIN-DESTROY]

On dismiss:
1. Unsubscribe all child widgets from the bus
2. Destroy the `CTkToplevel` window
3. Publish dismiss event to the bus
4. Template returns to DORMANT
5. If a lower-priority trigger was pending: evaluate it now (it may fire)

---

## 4. Priority Replacement [CXC-RWIN-PRIORITY]

Same priority stack as the tab controller:

| Priority | Meaning | Example |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `critical` | Safety or fault — demands immediate attention | Hull breach, FTL offline, E-stop activated |
| `high` | Important operational alert | Ammo low, overtemp warning, session error |
| `normal` | Informational popup | New device discovered, vote opened |
| `low` | Nice-to-know | Non-urgent status change, background task complete |

**Replacement rule:** A trigger with priority strictly greater than the current
window's priority replaces it. Equal or lower priority is dropped.

**Exception:** If the current window was triggered by a condition that has since
cleared (the auto-dismiss conditions are now met, but the operator hasn't
acknowledged yet), ANY priority trigger replaces it — the stale window yields.

---

## 5. Bus Events [CXC-RWIN-EVENTS]

The reactive window module publishes lifecycle events:

### 5.1 Window Opened

```
PUBLISH composer/reactive_window/opened
{
  "template_id": "ammo_alert",
  "trigger_namespace": "ammo_inventory",
  "trigger_value": 15,
  "priority": "high",
  "timestamp": 1742169600
}
```

### 5.2 Window Dismissed

```
PUBLISH composer/reactive_window/dismissed
{
  "template_id": "ammo_alert",
  "dismiss_reason": "auto_clear",     # "auto_clear", "timeout", "acknowledge", "replaced"
  "replaced_by": null,                 # or template_id of the replacement
  "duration_seconds": 45,
  "timestamp": 1742169645
}
```

### 5.3 Operator Acknowledge

```
PUBLISH composer/reactive_window/acknowledged
{
  "template_id": "ammo_alert",
  "timestamp": 1742169640
}
```

These events are on the internal bus. Other modules (Intelligence, toolbar,
logging) can subscribe to track operator awareness of alerts.

---

## 6. ComposeUX Design Integration [CXC-RWIN-DESIGN]

### 6.1 Reactive Window Panel [CXC-RWIN-PANEL]

In the ComposeUX editor, reactive windows are NOT on the canvas (they're
dormant — no visual presence). Instead, a **Reactive Windows panel** lists
all defined templates:

```
┌─ Reactive Windows ──────────────────────┐
│                                          │
│  ▶ ammo_alert        [high]    ⚡ < 20  │
│  ▶ ftl_fault         [critical] ⚡ == offline │
│  ▶ device_discovered [normal]  ⚡ discovery │
│                                          │
│  [+ Add Window]                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Clicking a template opens it in a design sub-canvas where the operator
designs the window's widget layout. The trigger and dismiss conditions
are configured in the property editor.

### 6.2 Preview [CXC-RWIN-PREVIEW]

The preview module can simulate reactive window triggers. The state injector
publishes a value that fires the trigger, and the preview renders the window
as a floating frame within the preview area.

### 6.3 Intelligence API [CXC-RWIN-INTEL]

An Intelligence can define reactive windows via the standard command taxonomy:

```
PUBLISH composer/reactive_window/template_added
{
  "template": {
    "id": "ammo_alert",
    "title": "Ammunition Status",
    "trigger": [{"namespace": "ammo_inventory", "condition": "< 20"}],
    "dismiss": {"auto": [{"namespace": "ammo_inventory", "condition": ">= 50"}]},
    "priority": "high",
    "widgets": [...]
  }
}
```

---

## 7. Serialization [CXC-RWIN-SERIAL]

Reactive window templates are serialized in the `.compose.yaml` under the
`reactive_windows` key (top-level, alongside `modules`, `widgets`, `bindings`):

```yaml
compose_version: "0.1"
metadata:
  name: "Tactical Dashboard"

modules:
  - id: main_panel
    # ... normal module layout ...

reactive_windows:
  - id: ammo_alert
    # ... template definition ...
```

The `reactive_windows` key is optional. Designs without reactive windows
omit it entirely (backward compatible).

---

## 8. Relationship to Tab Controller [CXC-RWIN-TAB]

The reactive window and tab controller share:
- Condition evaluation syntax (same expressions)
- Priority resolution model (same stack)
- User-hold concept (tab: 5s after click; window: acknowledge button)
- Bus event publication pattern

They differ in:
- **Lifecycle**: Tabs are persistent, windows are ephemeral
- **Multiplicity**: Multiple tabs coexist, only one reactive window at a time
- **Scope**: Tabs are children of a TabWidget in the layout, windows are top-level
- **Design surface**: Tabs are on the canvas, windows are in a separate panel

The condition evaluation engine SHOULD be shared code — both the TabWidget
and the ReactiveWindowModule use the same `evaluate_condition(namespace, value, condition_expr)` function.

---

## 9. Compliance [CXC-RWIN-COMPLIANCE]

A compliant reactive window implementation MUST:

1. **[RWIN-C1]** Evaluate trigger conditions on every received bus message
2. **[RWIN-C2]** Enforce one-window-at-a-time with priority replacement
3. **[RWIN-C3]** Instantiate live widgets that subscribe to the bus (not static snapshots)
4. **[RWIN-C4]** Support all three dismiss paths: auto-clear, timeout, acknowledge
5. **[RWIN-C5]** Publish lifecycle events (opened, dismissed, acknowledged)
6. **[RWIN-C6]** Destroy window and unsubscribe widgets on dismiss
7. **[RWIN-C7]** Serialize templates in `.compose.yaml` under `reactive_windows`
8. **[RWIN-C8]** Yield stale windows (auto-dismiss conditions met) to any priority trigger

